      "text": "To convert the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) from rectangular coordinates to spherical coordinates, we need to find \\(\\rho\\), \\(\\theta\\), and \\(\\phi\\).\n\nThe formulas for converting from rectangular coordinates \\((x, y, z)\\) to spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\) are:\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\), we can substitute \\(x = 0\\), \\(y = -3\\sqrt{3}\\), and \\(z = 3\\) into these formulas.\n\n1. Calculate \\(\\rho\\):\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2} = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(\\theta\\):\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right)\n\\]\nSince the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3})\\) lies on the negative \\(y\\)-axis, \\(\\theta\\) is \\(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\) (or 270 degrees).\n\n3. Calculate \\(\\phi\\):\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the spherical coordinates are:\n\\[\n(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) = \\left(6, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(6, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}\n\\]",
