      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To convert the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) from rectangular coordinates to spherical coordinates, we need to determine the values of \\(\\rho\\), \\(\\theta\\), and \\(\\phi\\).\n\n1. **Calculate \\(\\rho\\):**\n   The radial distance \\(\\rho\\) is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   \\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\n   \\]\n   Substituting the given coordinates \\(x = 0\\), \\(y = -3\\sqrt{3}\\), and \\(z = 3\\):\n   \\[\n   \\rho = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate \\(\\theta\\):**\n   The azimuthal angle \\(\\theta\\) is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Substituting the given coordinates \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = -3\\sqrt{3}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Since the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) lies on the negative \\(y\\)-axis, \\(\\theta\\) is \\(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\) (or \\(\\frac{7\\pi}{2}\\), but we typically use the principal value within \\([0, 2\\pi)\\)).\n\n3. **Calculate \\(\\phi\\):**\n   The polar angle \\(\\phi\\) is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   \\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Substituting the values of \\(z = 3\\) and \\(\\rho = 6\\):\n   \\[\n   \\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n   The value of \\(\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\) is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(6, \\frac{7\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3})}\n\\]",
